ation/scope trade-off excerpt in the retrieved set. Evidence available: Learning to Infer Program Sketches combines a learned sketch generator with explicit symbolic search: the RNN sketch generator predicts sketches from specs, and an enumerative synthesizer fills holes; the authors also state a trade-off that fixed/static sketch grammars are too restrictive, so they introduce a flexible, domain-general sketch grammar and self-supervision to let the model learn how much to rely on neural pattern recognition versus symbolic search. For Program Synthesis with a REPL, the paper combines learned policy/value with execution feedback by using a REPL to execute partial programs during SMC search, reweighting candidates by the value of the resulting partial state and searching in semantic space. Missing aspect: a direct evaluation/scope trade-off stated by the REPL authors (for example, a limitation of requiring a hand-designed partial-program executor or a restricted language setting) is not yet supported by the retrieved excerpts, so I cannot fully satisfy the “for each paper, report one evaluation or scope trade-off” requirement from the current evidence.", "raw": "{\"action\":\"answer\",\"query\":\"Compare Learning to Infer Program Sketches with Program Synthesis with a REPL. How does each combine learning with symbolic search or execution feedback? For each paper, report one evaluation or scope trade-off stated by its authors and explain how it qualifies the method.\",\"title\":\"Learning to Infer Program Sketches; Program Synthesis with a REPL\",\"missing_aspects\":[],\"reason\":\"The retrieved excerpts support both methods and one trade-off for Learning to Infer Program Sketches, but the REPL paper lacks a focused evaluation/scope trade-off excerpt in the retrieved set. Evidence available: Learning to Infer Program Sketches combines a learned sketch generator with explicit symbolic search: the RNN sketch generator predicts sketches from specs, and an enumerative syn